#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4243" :question/problem "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is\n\n$$\na_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{64} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{8^{2}} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{-2} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3} & > 2000000 \\\\ 8^{n - 3} & > 1000000 \\\\ \\log (8^{n - 3}) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ (n - 3)\\log (8) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ n - 3 & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4244" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16$, and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16} = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2^{4} \\cdot 2^{1 - n} \\\\\n&= 2^{5 - n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{5 - n} & < 0.0000001 \\\\ \\log (2^{5 - n}) & < \\log (0.0000001) \\\\ 5 - n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) \\\\ -n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) - 5 \\\\ n & > 5 - (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac {\\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "154a5006-6c4f-58c7-a228-ccbefabd5050" :question/math-academy-id "e-4245" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54$, and the common ratio is $3$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000$?" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and $r= 3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[3pt] a_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1} \\\\[3pt] 54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3 \\\\[3pt] 54&=27a_1 \\\\[3pt] a_1&=2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} & > 1000 \\\\ 3^{n - 1} & > 500 \\\\ \\log (3)^{n - 1} & > \\log (500) \\\\ (n - 1)\\log (3) & > \\log (500) \\\\ n - 1 & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the First Term Is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98af2b7e-b501-50b4-9ff1-67ef6405e85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090306} {:answer/id #uuid "9b59777f-2b7f-5144-8388-3b195f6d0e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $13$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090307} {:answer/id #uuid "a7226361-d652-5ef0-8f65-dd0f631aa6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090308} {:answer/id #uuid "08f8122f-3138-592b-a3dc-3baba00ee74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $14$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090309} {:answer/id #uuid "364d0936-9952-5d0b-a27d-353a972d10cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7226361-d652-5ef0-8f65-dd0f631aa6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b26187a-009e-5acc-9d13-e1a43587b264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090305}] :que